{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NGSbei8MCK6ocG8pqeuKfJ","type":"tool_result","content":"     603 test/mutations-delete.test.ts\n     208 test/cli-process-mutations-delete.test.ts\n     536 test/mutations.test.ts\n     171 test/cli-process-mutations.test.ts\n    1518 total\n4:// the Epic 01 read-back (TC-6.1); its own forms drop while the turn and\n6:// exactly at the chunk (TC-6.2). Prompt protection routes whole-exchange\n7:// intent to the turn surface (TC-6.3), where deleteTurn removes the turn\n8:// and all its messages from reads and chunk membership (TC-6.4), re-derives\n10:// boundary (TC-6.5, architecture risk: the captureInputs assertion proves\n13:// (TC-6.6, architecture risk). Refusals — open turn, missing id, double\n15:// (TC-6.7). The CLI parity leg (TC-6.8) lives in\n18:import { afterEach, beforeEach, describe, expect, it } from \"vitest\";\n177:describe(\"TC-6.1 / AC-6.1: a deleted message leaves reads and membership; its events remain\", () => {\n178:  it(\"deleting a tool-result message removes it from message reads and turn membership, not from event read-back\", async () => {\n206:describe(\"TC-6.2 / AC-6.2 (architecture risk): delete drops own forms, re-queues upward, stops at the chunk\", () => {\n207:  it(\"the deleted message's forms are gone, its turn and chunk re-queue, chunk 2 is byte-stable\", async () => {\n282:describe(\"TC-6.3 / AC-6.3 (architecture risk): prompt protection routes to the turn surface\", () => {\n283:  it(\"deleting a turn-initiating prompt is refused naming the turn and the turns-delete path; nothing changes\", async () => {\n303:describe(\"TC-6.4 / AC-6.4: turn delete removes the turn and its messages from reads; events remain\", () => {\n304:  it(\"a three-message turn leaves turn reads, message reads, and chunk membership; event read-back is byte-stable\", async () => {\n348:describe(\"TC-6.5 / AC-6.5 (architecture risk): the chunk re-derives from remaining members; bounda